<title>GERMANY: German court says withholding tax constitutional.</title>
<headline>German court says withholding tax constitutional.</headline>
<dateline>MUNICH, Germany 1997-02-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Imposing withholding tax on interest income does not violate Germany's constitution, the country's finance court decided on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The court said a law passed in 1992 that changed the way interest income is taxed in 1993 was constitutional thereby, overruling an earlier decision by a Munich court.</p>
<p>The ruling is not related to plans by Chancellor Helmut Kohl's coalition government to reform the German tax system by cutting a net 30 billion marks ($17.8 billion) in income and company taxes in 1998 and 1999.</p>
<p>-- Bonn newsroom, 49-228-26097 150 ($ = 1.686 German Marks)</p>
